WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
- We have an incredible show today, y'all.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.260
Joining us is one of the funniest,

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.000
most groundbreaking comedians

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.790
in the history of American
television and film.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.910
She's won a Tony, a Grammy, six Emmys,

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.025
and seven Golden Globes,
'cause why the hell not?

00:00:15.025 --> 00:00:15.858
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:15.858 --> 00:00:16.691
Winning all of them.

00:00:16.691 --> 00:00:19.740
She was awarded the
Presidential Medal of Freedom,

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.710
as well as the Mark Twain
prize for American Humor.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.530
Tonight, on NBC, you can
actually watch her tribute.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:29.130
It's called "Carol Burnett, 90
Years of Laughter and Love."

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:30.540
It's incredible.
(audience applauding)

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:35.540
And today happens to be her 90th birthday.

00:00:35.805 --> 00:00:39.385
Everybody, give it up for
the iconic Carol Burnett!

00:00:39.385 --> 00:00:44.385
(audience cheers and claps)
(carefree music)

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:56.033
(audience continues to cheer and clap)

00:01:01.405 --> 00:01:06.322
(audience continues cheering and clapping)

00:01:08.162 --> 00:01:08.995
Hello.

00:01:10.927 --> 00:01:12.267
- Thank you.
(audience cheering)

00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:14.434
- [Kelly] Keep that going!

00:01:16.277 --> 00:01:18.194
- Stop...
- Oh my gosh.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.283
- Thank you.

00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:21.830
- We're so excited you're here.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.070
- [Carol] Oh, I'm so excited to be here.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.401
- I feel like my show just
made it, that you're on it.

00:01:26.401 --> 00:01:28.830
(Carol and Kelly laugh)
(audience cheers)

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:29.910
- We're very excited.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.560
Today is a very special day.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.150
Obviously, happy birthday.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:33.983
- Thank you.
(audience cheers again)

00:01:33.983 --> 00:01:35.850
- It was my birthday two days ago.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.102
- Your birthday is the 24th.
- We're both Tauruses.

00:01:38.102 --> 00:01:38.935
24th, yeah.

00:01:38.935 --> 00:01:39.768
- I'm the 26th.
- 26th

00:01:39.768 --> 00:01:42.960
- You and also Shirley
McClain and Barbara Streisand.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.660
- Don't you think I know it, girl? Yes.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.640
I was very excited as a kid.
- And your birthday, yeah.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.170
There's a lot of Tauruses killing it.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.820
Just saying. We're pretty cool.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:51.960
We're both from Texas as well.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.970
- [Carol] That's right, yes.
- Yes, was it San Antonio?

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:54.840
- San Antonio.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:55.673
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:01:55.673 --> 00:01:57.000
- Do you miss anything?

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.340
Because you were young when
you moved to Hollywood, right?

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.256
- It was back in the covered wagon days.

00:02:01.256 --> 00:02:03.333
- (laughs) The Oregon Trail.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.550
- I went to Davy Crockett Grammar School.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:09.420
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.280
- In San Antonio, yeah.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.270
- [Kelly] I like San Antonio.
- He was alive then.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:14.243
- (laughing) No, he was not.

00:02:15.331 --> 00:02:16.810
(Carol and Kelly laugh)

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:18.900
- I miss the queso.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.760
I miss chips and queso from Texas.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:21.900
That's my favorite thing.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.460
- The best Mexican food, ever.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:24.990
- The Tex-Mex, yes.

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:26.220
- Yeah.
- I know.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.170
- That's the first food I remember eating.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.970
- [Kelly] Yeah. Cu it-
- Enchiladas.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.951
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh.
- Yeah.

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:33.470
- My mouth just watered.
- And then I had my milk.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.590
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:35.490
- Then I had my bottle.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.290
- Yeah. A little bottle of milk, yes.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.770
- So everybody, Carol has given
the world so many good times

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.350
and so many laughs over the years that we,

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.780
as a selfish gift, more
to ourselves than her,

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.510
want to look back and relive
a few of those moments.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:51.780
So, we have compiled a
collection of memories.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.310
This is Carol's scrapbook.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.540
A look at 90 years entertaining the world.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.188
(audience cheers)
(cheerful music)

00:03:00.188 --> 00:03:01.830
Okay, so let's start at the beginning.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:02.663
Here we go.

00:03:02.663 --> 00:03:05.580
So, Carol was doing characters
from a very young age.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.490
In fact, is it true that a
neighbor once got upset with you

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:09.600
because he heard...

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:10.433
- Oh! (Carol laughs)

00:03:10.433 --> 00:03:11.638
It sounded like a real radio station?

00:03:11.638 --> 00:03:13.770
- Well, I lived with my grandmother.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:14.790
She raised me.
- Yeah.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.010
- And we had this one room apartment.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.090
- Yeah.
- And so,

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.760
it butted up against another building,

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.290
another apartment building.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.890
So, I would sit on the couch
and I would open the window

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:28.350
and I would pretend to be a radio show.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.183
- Yeah.

00:03:29.183 --> 00:03:31.620
- You know, I was about
10 or 11 or whatever.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.230
And I would say, "And
now ladies and gentlemen,

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.270
you're going to hear from a young lady

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.580
who's going to sing acapella,

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:41.040
without any orchestra accompaniment."

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.770
And then I would sing and
pretend to be this, right?

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:48.247
And finally somebody from
the building next door said,

00:03:48.247 --> 00:03:50.808
"Turn that damned thing off."

00:03:50.808 --> 00:03:52.110
(Kelly and the audience laugh)

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:55.320
I was thrilled! (Kelly laughs louder)

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:56.580
I mean, they believed it.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.740
They really thought it was a radio show.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.390
- You were that good.
(Carol and Kelly laugh)

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:01.640
You're like, "Thank you."

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.640
Okay, let's move on to your
late teens and twenties.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:06.473
So, here we go.

00:04:06.473 --> 00:04:08.927
You started acting in college,
in Los Angeles, right?

00:04:08.927 --> 00:04:09.960
- [Carol] Right. UCLA

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.640
- [Kelly] How did you
end up going to New York?

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:12.473
What made the move?

00:04:12.473 --> 00:04:13.306
When did you make the move?

00:04:13.306 --> 00:04:18.306
- Well, I was in a musical
comedy workshop at UCLA

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.370
and we did a scene.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:24.630
I did a scene from "Annie
Get Your Gun" as a final.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.750
They were gonna grade us on
it, but there was an audience.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.860
And afterwards this gentleman
and his wife came up to me,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.660
and they said, "We liked you very much,"

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.490
and, "What do you wanna
do with your life?"

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.730
And I said, "Well, someday
I wanna go to New York,

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.610
and be on the stage, and
be in musical comedy,

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:42.695
- Yeah.

00:04:42.695 --> 00:04:44.550
like Mary Martin and Ethel Merman."

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:45.383
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:45.383 --> 00:04:47.160
- The gentleman said, "Well,
why aren't you there now?"

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.110
And I said, "Well, you know."

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.040
We were...

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.060
- Costs money.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.800
- [Carol] Yeah. And we were poor.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:53.880
- Yeah.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.090
- And I said, "But someday
I hope to save up enough."

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:00.030
And he said, "I'll lend you the money."

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.010
And so he did.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.180
- A complete stranger?

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:06.360
- Yep. And I thought,
"What, is he kidding?"

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:07.950
And his wife said, "No, he'll..."

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:09.490
And he lent me

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.020
one thousand dollars.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:17.280
And he said, "Pay it back when
you can, within five years.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:18.270
Stipulations:

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:19.893
Don't ever reveal my name.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.970
You must use the money
to go to New York on.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:25.500
And if you are successful,

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.679
you must promise to help
other people out too."

00:05:28.679 --> 00:05:30.150
(audience applauds)
- What a beautiful lesson,

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:31.950
right off the bat.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.240
- So I did.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:37.830
I took the money. I went to
New York and I got lucky.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:39.379
(Carol chuckles)

00:05:39.379 --> 00:05:42.547
- I think talent had
something to do with it, yeah.

00:05:42.547 --> 00:05:43.380
(Kelly laughs)

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:44.310
(audience cheers)
- I paid him back.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.496
- That's incredible.
- Yep.

00:05:46.496 --> 00:05:48.300
- So you were... I love this.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.770
I'm gonna switch from this beautiful thing

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:51.330
to you were fired.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:52.920
- Oh.
- From an early job you had

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.140
as an usher, at a theater
on Hollywood Boulevard.

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:55.973
- [Carol] Right.

00:05:55.973 --> 00:05:57.510
- So what happened? How did you get fired?

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:58.500
- Well, what happened...

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.830
It was 65 cents an hour
that I was getting,

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:02.850
and I was an usher.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.150
It was my part-time job
while it was summer,

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:09.000
and I was still a UCLA student.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.530
And we had to wear these funny outfits.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:18.530
I had epaulets, and harem
pants, and a fez hat and all.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:22.200
And I was standing in
front of aisle two's door

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:23.370
this one night.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:26.580
And back then they would
just show the movie

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:27.600
over and over and over.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:31.890
So, people would come in
in the middle of the movie.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.360
Yeah, that's what they did back then.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:34.193
It was stupid.

00:06:34.193 --> 00:06:35.026
- What?
- Yeah.

00:06:36.039 --> 00:06:37.710
And then they would sit
there, and then they'd wait,

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.290
and then they'd watch it and
it would start over again.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.850
And they'd say, "Oh, this
is where we came in."

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:42.900
And then they'd leave, you know?

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:44.400
So anyway,

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:45.233
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:06:45.233 --> 00:06:47.700
- we were showing an
Alfred Hitchcock movie.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.800
- Yeah.
- Now there's a thriller.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:52.830
And it was "Strangers on a Train."

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:56.130
It was the last five minutes
and it was gonna start again.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:59.310
This couple came in
and wanted to be seated

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.500
in the last five minutes

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:04.350
of an Alfred Hitchcock Movie!
- Spoil alert.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:05.183
- [Carol] I mean, you know....

00:07:05.183 --> 00:07:08.220
So I said, "Oh, please
no. It'll ruin it for you.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.960
Go get popcorn. Go to the bathroom.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.540
But, it'll start all over,

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.040
and you should see it from the beginning."

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:18.390
Well, the manager came up and
said, "What's going on here?"

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:21.420
And this woman said, "She
won't let us sit down."

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.880
And I said, "But, Mr. Batten..."

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:24.787
That was his name.

00:07:24.787 --> 00:07:27.600
"Mr. Batten, please? It'll..."

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:28.440
He said, "Burnett."

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:29.788
I said, "Yes?"

00:07:29.788 --> 00:07:31.817
He went, "keech."

00:07:31.817 --> 00:07:33.180
Literally, he went "keech,"

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:37.410
and he ripped off my
epaulets. (Kelly laughs)

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.330
And I was fired on the spot.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:40.530
- Oh my god!

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:44.223
- Well, nice, nice ending to this story.

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:47.047
Several years later when they said,

00:07:47.047 --> 00:07:49.729
"Where do you want your star
on Hollywood Boulevard?"

00:07:49.729 --> 00:07:51.300
(audience groans)
- Oh my God.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.032
If that's not a joke- (Kelly laughs)

00:07:53.032 --> 00:07:57.448
(audience claps and cheers)

00:07:57.448 --> 00:07:58.410
Oh my god.

00:07:58.410 --> 00:07:59.460
You got 'em in the end.

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:01.020
- I did. (Carol and Kelly laugh)

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:02.310
- So, let's turn the page

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.350
to Carol's first big success on TV.

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.340
Here we go.

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:07.020
So, you were on "The Garry Moore Show,"

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:08.040
- Yes.
- in New York,

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.510
which was hugely popular at the time.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:11.460
So, what do you remember
about that experience?

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:13.650
- Oh, well, first of all

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:16.080
there wasn't a nicer human being

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.150
that I had ever worked
for than Gary Moore.

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:19.110
- [Kelly] That doesn't always happen.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:19.943
- No.
- Yeah.

00:08:19.943 --> 00:08:22.650
- There were times when we
would be reading the script

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:23.850
for that week,

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:25.920
and Durward Kirby was also.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:27.780
He was the second banana on the show.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:28.980
- Yeah.
- That's what we were.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:30.750
They call them second bananas.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:34.020
And if Garry had a punchline,
or a joke or something,

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.150
he'd look at it, he'd say, "You know what?

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:37.950
Give this to Carol, or
give this to Durward.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:40.350
They can say it funnier than I can."

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.330
That's the kind of man he was.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:43.200
- He was confident.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.033
- Absolutely

00:08:44.033 --> 00:08:45.783
- Not insecure in his talent.
- Not at all.

00:08:45.783 --> 00:08:46.710
- It's people that are
insecure, I feel like,

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:47.560
that get in their own way.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:48.960
- Yeah, yeah.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:50.790
- Moving on to 1967,

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:53.977
which is when Carol started
her own iconic variety show,

00:08:53.977 --> 00:08:55.467
"The Carol Burnett Show."

00:08:56.995 --> 00:08:57.828
It's incredible.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:08:59.963
I didn't know this,

00:08:59.963 --> 00:09:02.040
but you were originally
supposed to be in a sitcom

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.210
when you came to Hollywood?

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:05.610
So, how did you end up doing
a variety sketch show instead?

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:09.630
- Well, I had a contract that
was very unusual that said,

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:14.630
if I wanted to do a musical
comedy variety show,

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:17.940
all I had to do was push a button,

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:20.700
and CBS would have to put it on the air

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:24.810
for 30 one hour shows,
whether they wanted to or not.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:26.220
- What a great contract.

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:27.420
- I know. (audience laughs)

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:29.550
- Yeah.
- So I thought...

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:31.980
I pushed the button, and I called,

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:33.180
and they said "What?"

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:35.220
I said, "I want to do the variety show."

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:38.490
And they said they'd
forgotten about the contract.

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:39.323
- You didn't. (Kelly laughs)

00:09:39.323 --> 00:09:40.156
- I didn't.

00:09:40.156 --> 00:09:41.880
And I said, "You know where..."

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:45.600
And he said, "You know, Carol,

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.630
comedy variety is a man's game."

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:49.680
- Can't hear that enough.

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:50.760
- [Carol] Yeah, it's a man's game.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:51.810
It's Dean Martin.

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:53.610
It's Sid Caesar.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:55.774
It's Jackie Gleason, Milton Burr.

00:09:55.774 --> 00:10:00.210
Literally, he said,
"It's not for you gals."

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:02.100
- Wow!
- So I said, "Well,

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:03.930
this gal wants to.
- Makes you wanna do it more.

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:04.770
- Totally.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:06.150
They had a sitcom.

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:08.760
He said, "Oh, we've got this
great sitcom you could do,

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:10.807
called "Here's Agnes."

00:10:10.807 --> 00:10:12.690
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:15.450
- And you'll be able to turn
everyone on, yeah. (laughs)

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.092
- Here's Agnes.

00:10:17.092 --> 00:10:18.630
(Kelly and audience laugh,
then Carol joins in)

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:20.940
I said, "I don't wanna
be Agnes every week."

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:21.870
- Got this great role.

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:22.920
- No thank you.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:24.720
I want to have a rep company.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:25.553
I wanna have music.

00:10:25.553 --> 00:10:26.386
I want to have dancers.

00:10:26.386 --> 00:10:27.990
I want to have guest stars.

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:29.550
- Yeah.
- I want to have all kinds...

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:31.860
They had to put us on the air.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:32.880
- You know what?

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.370
I don't know if I've
ever heard of a contract

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:37.483
really working in an
artist's favor before.

00:10:37.483 --> 00:10:39.240
(everyone laughs)

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.030
That might be the first time, yeah.

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.220
So your show really pushed
the envelope though.

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:46.620
Did the network ever
try to change something?

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:47.670
They had to have, right?

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:49.320
Something that you wanted to do?

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:50.430
Did they...

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:52.054
- They left us alone, pretty much.

00:10:52.054 --> 00:10:52.887
- What?

00:10:52.887 --> 00:10:54.990
- Yeah. There was only one time

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:58.260
that the sensors had
something to say to us.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:10:59.400
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:03.270
- Harvey was talking to
me as an interviewer,

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:05.965
and I'm a nudist behind a...

00:11:05.965 --> 00:11:07.037
- [Kelly] I know this one, yeah.

00:11:07.037 --> 00:11:09.450
- behind a fence, you know?

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:10.860
And so, I'm bare shouldered,

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:14.363
and I have bare legs and
high top sneakers on.

00:11:14.363 --> 00:11:15.990
- And you're spraying for
mosquitoes or something.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:16.823
- You remember!

00:11:16.823 --> 00:11:18.697
And so his line was,

00:11:18.697 --> 00:11:22.830
"So, what do you nudists
do for recreation?"

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:25.380
And for my line, I said,
"Well, we have dances

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:27.090
every Saturday night."

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:29.760
And then he said, "How
do you nudists dance?"

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.130
And my line was, "Very carefully."

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:33.990
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:36.190
They thought that was too dirty.

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:37.440
- Oh...

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.690
- Yeah, the network thought,
"Oh, you can't say that.

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:41.866
You have to go with something else."

00:11:41.866 --> 00:11:44.467
So this is what we came up with:

00:11:44.467 --> 00:11:46.927
"We have dances every Saturday night."

00:11:46.927 --> 00:11:49.447
"Well, how do you nudists dance?"

00:11:49.447 --> 00:11:50.730
"Cheek to cheek."

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:52.200
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.300
- Which actually, is even better.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:55.660
- Of course!

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:57.870
And they bought that.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:00.260
- We have a clip of that,
because I love this one.

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:01.620
- Oh, you do?
- So, let's show it.

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:03.820
(audience on original video laughs)

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:05.030
- [Harvey] Tell me, do you
have any kind of a motto

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:08.153
or sss...

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:13.363
(audience on original video laughs)

00:12:17.227 --> 00:12:18.867
- I got the little devil.

00:12:18.867 --> 00:12:21.740
(audience continues to laugh)

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:23.880
(tinkling musical run up a glockenspiel)

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:26.580
- [Harvey] I was asking, do
you have any kind of a motto

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:27.840
or slogan?

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:29.880
- Oh, no. But we have a camp song.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:30.713
- [Harvey] Oh? What's it called?

00:12:30.713 --> 00:12:32.760
- On a Clear Day You Can See forever.

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:35.070
(audience on original video laughs)

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:36.510
- [Harvey] Tell me, what
kind of a social life

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:37.343
do you have here?

00:12:37.343 --> 00:12:39.120
- Oh, same as anywheres else.

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:42.000
We have movies, parties, dances...

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:43.050
- Dances?
- Uh huh.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:44.520
- [Harvey] How do nudists dance?

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:45.910
- Cheek to cheek.

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:47.230
(audience on original video laughs)

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:50.330
(studio audience laughs)
- I love it.

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:53.940
I have said this so often on this show,

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:58.020
but comedians are my
favorite actors, generally.

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:00.810
Because, I don't know what it is.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:02.100
One, you're really witty.

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:03.900
Usually highly intelligent individuals.

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:05.250
But also,

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:08.100
I think comedians, you're
able to go really deep.

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:09.780
You're able to go to the depths

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:11.610
where some people are afraid to go,

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:13.350
because you're willing to
do it for either a joke,

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:14.430
or you're willing to do it...

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:17.280
So that willingness, I think,
makes y'all better actors.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.380
It makes you far more captivating.

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:24.380
- I actually, it sounds
patronizing on my part,

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:28.650
but I think comedians can do drama easier

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:30.510
than a dramatic actor can do comedy.

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:32.080
- No, I... One hundred percent.

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:33.159
- Because of timing.
- Yes.

00:13:33.159 --> 00:13:35.490
- You know, that's the important thing.

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:37.260
- And I do think that there's something,

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:39.180
'cuss one of my first jobs was, actually,

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:40.500
waitressing at a comedy club,

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:43.440
and it was interesting
to see them on stage,

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:45.660
and then when all the patrons left, right?

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:49.240
And then you saw that some
of them could be quite dark.

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:50.400
- [Carol] Right.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.440
- But highly intelligent,
very introverted.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:54.240
And it was like the opposite
of what you saw on stage.

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:56.790
- We would get dark
when we did the family.

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:58.590
- Yeah.
- With Eunice and Mama and Ed?

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:01.020
- Loved....
- Those were...

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:02.993
There were no jokes in that.

00:14:02.993 --> 00:14:04.620
- That's interesting.
- It was all character driven.

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:08.820
And one time we were doing
a scene, rehearsing it,

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:10.767
and I don't remember
if it was Harvey or me,

00:14:10.767 --> 00:14:15.720
but one of us said, "Just for fun,

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:18.870
let's do it without all the
accents and all the crazy,"

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:21.570
the way we would normally say a line.

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:22.403
- Yeah.

00:14:22.403 --> 00:14:23.880
- So we played it totally straight.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:25.099
- Yeah.

00:14:25.099 --> 00:14:25.932
- There wasn't one laugh.

00:14:25.932 --> 00:14:26.765
- Yeah.
- Not one laugh.

00:14:26.765 --> 00:14:28.230
It was so heavy.

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:31.170
But then we put the spin
on it with these accents

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:32.370
and the way we talked
to each other, and all.

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:34.320
- Yes.
- And then it became funny.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:35.364
It was a great acting..
- It's one of my favorite

00:14:35.364 --> 00:14:36.690
things, ever.

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:37.523
Exercise.

00:14:37.523 --> 00:14:38.530
- Exercise, yeah.
- Yeah.

